From 2cce033e9d0d47dddbf7a17e25551bcc447504a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:50:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/15] PSCTRL: the settings surface - descriptor table,
 sub-ops, deferred apply

The emulator ships a table describing every control and the accessory
builds its dialog from it, so adding a switch later is one line here and
no .ACC rebuild. Sub-ops 3..11 on the existing PSCTRL feature: GETSTR,
SETINT, COUNT, DESCRIBE, SAVE, SETSTR, ACTION, LIST, SETAPI. Settings
share the PS_GETINT index namespace from PS_SET_BASE up, so a guest reads
a control's current value with the call it already has.

Correction to psctrl-live-settings-design.md: it assumed sub-ops 0-6 were
free. 0, 1 and 2 are already VERSION, GETINT and FX in the shipped
emulator and PSMON in the field calls 0 and 1, so the settings sub-ops
start at 3 and psctrl_settings.h supersedes the doc's numbering.

Three apply classes, enforced by PS_SETINT rather than merely drawn:

* LIVE - a plain int the emulator re-reads. The store is the change.

* DEFER - anything that reallocates or invalidates the translation cache.
  It CANNOT be applied in the handler: the caller is executing inside a
  translated block and alloc_cache() would vm_release() the code it is
  about to return into. The handler parks the request, calls
  jit_request_cpu_exit() and returns PS_R_DEFER; m68k_run_jit() drains it
  at the next block boundary, beside the existing stbox errand pump and
  at the same nesting level where the T0/T1/M path already calls
  flush_icache(3). Before do_specialties(), which can return out of the
  function.

* BOOT - the machine's shape. It goes into a shadow copy of the config
  and the dialog reads the shadow back, so a value you set stays set on
  screen without anything pretending it changed under the guest.

flush_icache_hard() is static in compemu_support_arm.cpp, so
psctrl_jit_flush_now() is added there rather than the caller reaching for
it; it must only ever be called from the boundary hook, and says so.

atari_natfeat.cpp gains psctrl_guest_write/read so psctrl_settings.cpp
never includes the memory banks - which also means it compiles on the
host for tests.
